WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.925
TERRY RYAN: Today we're talking
about Config Connector, Windows

00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:05.790
Server on GKE, and some
SRE advice on Toil.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.039
I'm Terry Ryan and welcome
to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:11.340
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.260
Config Connector is
a Kubernetes operator

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.920
that makes all GCP
resources behave as if they

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.810
were Kubernetes resources.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.450
The idea was to simplify
operations and resource

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.300
management by providing a single
way to manage your entire cloud

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.730
infrastructure
through Kubernetes.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.740
And now that idea has
graduated from beta

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.170
and is generally available.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.420
In other Kubernetes news,
Windows Server containers

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.600
can now run on Google
Kubernetes Engine in beta.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.150
If you're interested in
trying your containers on GKE,

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.160
we've got plenty of resources
to help you get started.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.130
And, of course, our
Kubernetes Community

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.800
deserves a huge shout-out for
bringing Kubernetes support

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.510
to Windows Server last year.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.730
You're the ones that
got this ball rolling.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.010
Our Site Reliability
Engineers, or SREs,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:56.470
have faced immense challenges
over Google's history

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.740
and every so often one
will share some advice.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.920
This week that advice centers
around identifying and tracking

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.480
Toil.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.000
Toil is any work that is
manual and repetitive,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.130
like handling quota requests.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.380
But to reduce Toil and
get back to engineering,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.820
you first need to
identify and measure it.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.560
So check out this breakdown
and let us know if it helps.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.400
Game Developer Conference
2020 is happening March 16

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.543
through the 20 in San Francisco.

00:01:22.543 --> 00:01:24.460
Whether you'll be there
in person or following

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.070
along online, we're stoked
to offer developer sessions

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.890
for using Google solutions
to build great games.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.900
If you want to follow
along, be sure to sign up

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.467
for announcements and
access to our livestream.

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.550
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.830
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:40.580
Any thoughts, ideas?

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.650
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.360
We'll see you in the cloud.

